WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:04.596
A refund ledger has a race.

2
00:00:04.596 --> 00:00:09.581
A test starts two threads, each makes one refund, and checks the total.

3
00:00:09.581 --> 00:00:11.125
It passes, every time.

4
00:00:11.333 --> 00:00:15.553
Starting a thread takes a while, and one refund is over in a moment.

5
00:00:15.553 --> 00:00:18.408
They never overlap, so the race never happens.

6
00:00:18.633 --> 00:00:20.389
How would you make them collide?

7
00:00:22.900 --> 00:00:26.875
Hold every thread at a start latch, then release them all at once.

8
00:00:27.100 --> 00:00:32.696
Use more threads than cores, and many operations each, so they overlap for a long time.

9
00:00:33.300 --> 00:00:35.400
Run many rounds with fresh state.

10
00:00:35.400 --> 00:00:38.327
Each round is one sample of the interleavings.

11
00:00:38.533 --> 00:00:40.838
After join, check an invariant.

12
00:00:40.838 --> 00:00:45.894
Eight threads try 2,000 one-cent refunds each, against 10,000 cents.

13
00:00:45.894 --> 00:00:48.943
A correct ledger approves exactly 10,000.

14
00:00:49.167 --> 00:00:51.733
The racy one approves 16,000.

15
00:00:51.967 --> 00:00:57.162
On the lesson's racy counter, the naive test failed at most 6 of 20 runs.

16
00:00:57.162 --> 00:00:59.297
The stress test failed all 20.

17
00:01:00.200 --> 00:01:01.142
Next, waiting.

18
00:01:01.142 --> 00:01:06.592
This test submits a payment, sleeps 50 milliseconds, then checks that it settled.

19
00:01:06.800 --> 00:01:09.558
On a busy CI machine, the work runs longer.

20
00:01:09.558 --> 00:01:13.341
The check comes too soon, and the test fails for no reason.

21
00:01:13.567 --> 00:01:16.944
In the lesson's runs, it failed about a quarter of the time.

22
00:01:17.167 --> 00:01:18.915
Instead, wait for the event.

23
00:01:18.915 --> 00:01:25.159
A latch counts down when the payment settles, and the test awaits it, with a deadline of 10 seconds.

24
00:01:25.367 --> 00:01:29.312
Fast when the work is fast, patient when the machine is slow.

25
00:01:29.312 --> 00:01:30.671
It failed zero times.

26
00:01:31.600 --> 00:01:34.935
In Java and Kotlin, it is the same four lines.

27
00:01:36.067 --> 00:01:38.906
Some races need millions of tries.

28
00:01:38.906 --> 00:01:42.331
jcstress, the OpenJDK harness, does that.

29
00:01:42.533 --> 00:01:46.259
Two actors increment one counter, and record what they read.

30
00:01:46.259 --> 00:01:49.736
It runs this millions of times, and counts each outcome.

31
00:01:49.967 --> 00:01:51.980
1, 2 and 2, 1 are acceptable.

32
00:01:51.980 --> 00:01:55.591
1, 1 means both read zero, so an increment was lost.

33
00:01:55.591 --> 00:01:59.480
It is labeled interesting: the race you are looking for.

34
00:01:59.700 --> 00:02:03.547
Label an outcome forbidden, and one sighting fails the test.

35
00:02:04.367 --> 00:02:04.962
The rule.

36
00:02:04.962 --> 00:02:11.975
Make threads collide on purpose, wait for events instead of time, and read a pass as: not found this time.

37
00:02:12.200 --> 00:02:13.243
Check yourself.

38
00:02:13.243 --> 00:02:18.387
A test starts two threads, each makes one refund, and it has never failed.

39
00:02:18.387 --> 00:02:19.708
Is the ledger safe?

40
00:02:22.233 --> 00:02:23.278
You can't tell.

41
00:02:23.278 --> 00:02:25.925
The refunds probably never overlapped.

42
00:02:25.925 --> 00:02:28.781
A pass only means this run found nothing.
